One more important point

The list isn't what makes the LLM choose a tool.

It's simply the API representation of:

"Here are all the tools you are allowed to request."

The model makes the choice based on the user's request and the tool descriptions/schema.

So:

tools = [calculate, divide, ...]
             ↓
        LLM sees them
             ↓
     chooses "divide"
             ↓
Python uses tool_registry
             ↓
     actual divide()

That's why we maintain both.

And this separation becomes very important when we later build a proper agent/tool architecture.


The model decides when it has finished generating its response.

The API reports that decision through:

finish_reason

For your model, you saw two important values:

tool_calls

Means:

"I'm not finished. I want my application to execute one or more tools and give me their results."

So your Python code does:

if finish_reason == "tool_calls":
    execute_tool()
    continue

And sends the conversation back to the LLM.

stop

Means:

"I'm finished generating this turn. I don't need another tool call."

So your application does:

elif finish_reason == "stop":
    final_answer = message["content"]
    break

The break is your program's decision to end the agent loop because the model indicated it was done.

But how does the LLM know it's done?

This is the really important part.

The model is generating an output based on the conversation it currently sees.

